 /*
- * Compile-only groundwork for the Apple T7000-family PCIe host controller.
+ * Apple T7000-family PCIe host controller -- read-only diagnostic driver.
  *
- * The real J81 ADT describes four ports, four port interrupts and twelve
- * register windows. Corellium's older H9P driver confirms the four-port
- * controller shape, but its register layout, SART and hard-coded PHY sequence
- * are not valid for T7000. The disabled device tree preserves J81's exact
- * resource ordering without naming the unresolved small-window roles. Keep
- * this driver inert until those roles, DART attachment and ADT-provided
- * tunables have been implemented.
+ * First hardware attempt at the write-capable version of this driver (which
+ * enabled port 1 and deasserted PERST# via a DT reset-gpios child node)
+ * hung with a black screen (backlight still on, no framebuffer console, no
+ * USB networking) on two separate boots, while the unmodified control
+ * payload booted normally from the same PongoOS/m1n1 pipeline moments
+ * later. That isolates the fault to this driver or its DT changes rather
+ * than the boot chain. Two real, unverified risks in that version: the
+ * reset-gpios pin identity (OIPG 179, reverse-engineered, never confirmed
+ * on real hardware) and the `power-domains = <&ps_pcie>` power-up this DT
+ * status flip triggers during probe, before this driver's own init runs.
+ *
+ * This version removes the write-capable enable sequence and the
+ * reset-gpios DT node entirely, and only reads and logs the current state
+ * of the same registers the enable sequence would have written, via
+ * dev_info() so it lands in dmesg. Both `dart_apcie1`/`pcie` are still
+ * flipped to status = "okay" -- that alone is what's needed to get this
+ * driver probed at all, and it's the same status flip either version needs.
+ * If this version also hangs before reaching a console, the fault is in
+ * that generic DT-status-triggered machinery (DART attach or the
+ * power-domain power-up), not in anything this driver's own code does. If
+ * it reaches dmesg cleanly, the fault is isolated to the register writes or
+ * the reset-gpios deassertion from the previous version.
+ *
+ * Register offsets and bit positions are traced directly from
+ * AppleT7000PCIe::_enablePortHardware and AppleEmbeddedPCIEPort::enableGated
+ * in the real J81 iOS 8.1 kernelcache -- not guessed. See
+ * docs/plans/2026-09-13-j81-wifi-pcie.md's "Enable-sequence checkpoint" and
+ * "port enable order" sections for the full evidence trail. Register
+ * windows 2/4/6/8 (of the ADT's 12 total) are confirmed unused by Apple's
+ * own host path and are intentionally left unmapped here too.
  */

